on this thanksgiving eve a story of loss  but also gratitude  a widow whose husband died of covid19  reunites with the healthcare workers who  took care of them  the i-team's vanessa murphy shares this  story you'll only see  right here on 8 news now  carissa hernandez is a widow with three  young daughters  arthur nazari her 42 year old husband  died in july  after contracting covet 19. and it  breaks my heart  to see that they're going to have to  grow up without a dad  when she first shared her story with the  eye team she expressed  grief and also gratitude for the medical  team  i just want them to know that you know  how how much that meant to me into my  family  after the i-team called mountain view  hospital they were virtually  reunited looking at your faces you guys  helped pull me through it these are the  intensive care unit nurses who cared for  arter in his final moments  and the cardiologist who treated him  earlier on  it was really a shock to see someone who  was otherwise healthy  get so sick and you know that really  stuck with me visitors aren't allowed  inside covet patients rooms  marisa muraoka grabbed an ipad so  carissa and her girls  could watch the final efforts to save  archer's life  and then say goodbye i promised my  husband when  he was in the hospital when he first got  sick that i would never leave his side  you enabled me to fulfill that promise  to him  diane darris held arthur's hand she was  telling him it's okay baby  you'll be okay you're just gonna go to  sleep for a little bit  and that last communication to me it was  priceless  so to me that was an honor to be there  kelly kohler did chest compressions  there was not a dry eye in that coat  we uh  actually had to  sorry  [Music]  it was hard having the kids in the  background  i can still remember them screaming  daddy daddy not my daddy  and those are definitely some things  that  will keep me up at night at times but i  i  i think i made the right i made the  right decision by not sheltering them  from that those  moments um to us were  part of what kept the resuscitation  going like the efforts to keep  doing as much as we can going but also  knowing when it was time and to  communicate that to carissa and saying  you know it's time to let him go  a nurse herself at a different hospital  carissa says  she believes these healthcare workers  did everything they could  because i know in my heart and soul that  they did  a meeting like this isn't the norm  karissa sharing how she and her  daughters are coping months later  these normally stoic health care workers  sharing how much  artur's death stays with them my heart  just just dropped and  i just want to say i'm sorry i just hope  that people who are experiencing loss  are able to move forward as gracefully  and with as much strength as you have  all of you are angels that day whether  you know it or not vanessa murphy  8 news now  tells the i-team mountain view hospital  waived arter's medical bills back in  august  she says corporate staff learned about  the circumstances  artur lost his job before he died he had  three young children and carissa  is a health care worker
 

